// TEST: AD Digamma Has No Derivative Rule (rejects)
// ERROR: BL5500
// ERROR-CONTAINS: has no derivative rule
import ad as ad
// ============================================================================
// This probe guards the SPLIT between "derivative is zero" and "derivative is
// unknown", not any particular intrinsic. Both readings show up in derivRule
// as `None`, and conflating them is the dangerous direction: an intrinsic with
// no rule that is silently treated as zero-derivative yields a WRONG gradient
// with that whole term missing and no diagnostic anywhere. Grad's
// zeroDerivIntrinsics lists the genuinely-zero ones (floor, ceil); everything
// else with no rule is refused here.
//
// It used to be lgamma standing at the frontier. lgamma is now differentiable
// -- derivRule maps it to digamma, which landed as an intrinsic -- so the
// probe MOVED UP one rung rather than being deleted: digamma's own derivative
// is trigamma, which the language does not have. See 015 for the other half,
// which pins the lgamma derivative that this file used to reject.
//
// If trigamma ever lands, do the same thing again: move this probe to
// trigamma and pin digamma's derivative in a positive test. Deleting it would
// silently re-merge the two readings of `None`.
// ============================================================================
function f(x: Float) -> Float = digamma(x) + x * x
let (fv, dfv) = ad.grad(f)(3.0)
